## [ISSUE] First access token of an offline_access request is encoded as ONLINE session type

**Severity:** HIGH - the session-type hint is wrong for exactly the case it is meant to optimise (online vs offline lookup, #37662).

### The Problem
```java
sessionType = userSession.isOffline() ? AccessTokenContext.SessionType.OFFLINE : AccessTokenContext.SessionType.ONLINE;
```
`initToken()` (TokenManager.java:1052-1054) calls this while the access token is being built. In every grant that can issue an offline token, the access token is created before the offline session exists:

- `OAuth2GrantTypeBase.createTokenResponse()` (line 110) calls `createClientAccessToken(...)` first, then `responseBuilder.generateRefreshToken()`.
- `TokenManager.AccessTokenResponseBuilder.generateRefreshToken()` (TokenManager.java:1210) throws `accessToken not set` if there is no access token yet. Only later, in `generateRefreshToken(boolean)`, does it call `sessionManager.createOrUpdateOfflineSession(...)` (TokenManager.java:1268).
- `ClientCredentialsGrantType` and `ResourceOwnerPasswordCredentialsGrantType` do the same: `generateAccessToken()` then `generateRefreshToken()`.

So the `userSession` passed to the encoder is the online session. The token gets `on`, even though the only long-lived session for it is offline.

### Theory / Proof
`OAuth2GrantTypeBase.java:115-120`: when the refresh token type is `Offline` and the client session has the `FIRST_OFFLINE_ACCESS` note, `session.sessions().removeUserSession(realm, userSession)` deletes the online session straight away. `ClientCredentialsGrantType` does the same (lines 144-147). After this, the token says `on` but no online session exists.

### What happens today vs. what will break
Nothing decodes the context yet, so nothing breaks today. The PR description says the follow-up (#37662) will use the session type to "lookup only correct session type". If that code trusts `on` and skips the offline fallback, every first access token from an `offline_access` request fails introspection/userinfo with "session not found" until the client refreshes. Tokens from later refreshes are fine, because the refresh path loads the offline session.

### Research
The same ordering problem is why `UserSessionUtil.findValidSession` and `TokenManager.validateToken` currently fall back from online to offline lookup: at issue time the code cannot always know which session will survive.

### Recommendation
Work out the session type from the requested scope as well as from `userSession.isOffline()`, e.g.:
```java
UserSessionModel userSession = clientSessionContext.getClientSession().getUserSession();
if (userSession.getPersistenceState() == UserSessionModel.SessionPersistenceState.TRANSIENT) {
    sessionType = AccessTokenContext.SessionType.TRANSIENT;
} else if (userSession.isOffline() || TokenUtil.isOfflineTokenRequested(clientSessionContext.getScopeString())) {
    sessionType = AccessTokenContext.SessionType.OFFLINE;
} else {
    sessionType = AccessTokenContext.SessionType.ONLINE;
}
```
Alternatively, re-encode the token id in `generateRefreshToken(boolean offline)` once the offline session is created. Add an integration test that asserts the decoded session type of the first access token from an `offline_access` authorization-code exchange.

## [ISSUE] `isAccessTokenId` matcher has an off-by-one index and inverted logic, so it never checks the grant type

**Severity:** MEDIUM - the only integration-level check of the new encoding does nothing, so regressions in grant-type encoding will not be caught.

### The Problem
```java
// Grant type shortcut starts at character 4th char and is 2-chars long
if (items[0].substring(3, 5).equals(expectedGrantShortcut)) return false;
return isUUID().matches(items[1]);
```
1. The layout is `st(0-1) tt(2-3) gt(4-5)`, as in `DefaultTokenContextEncoderProvider.getTokenContextFromTokenId`: `encodedContext.substring(4, 6)`. `substring(3, 5)` takes the last char of the token type plus the first char of the grant: for `onrtac` it returns `"ta"`.
2. The condition returns `false` when the value equals the expected shortcut. That is the opposite of what the matcher should do.

### Theory / Proof
The possible values of `substring(3,5)` are `t?` or `n?` (the second char of `rt`/`lt`/`un`), while the expected values are `ac`, `dg`, `rt`, `ci`. They never match, so the `return false` branch never runs and the matcher only checks the UUID. A token encoded with the wrong grant, e.g. `onrtna:<uuid>` for an authorization code exchange, passes `expectCodeToToken`.

### What happens today vs. what will break
Tests pass because the matcher is effectively `anything:UUID`. If someone fixes only the indices, every correct token fails, because the condition is inverted. If grant-type propagation regresses (see the unset `GRANT_TYPE` paths), no test catches it.

### Recommendation
```java
protected boolean matchesSafely(String item) {
    String[] items = item.split(":");
    if (items.length != 2 || items[0].length() != 6) return false;
    // Grant type shortcut is the last 2 characters of the encoded context
    if (!items[0].substring(4, 6).equals(expectedGrantShortcut)) return false;
    return isUUID().matches(items[1]);
}
```

## [ISSUE] New abstract `getShortcut()` makes custom grant providers fail server startup instead of degrading

**Severity:** LOW - this affects only deployments with custom grant types on an internal SPI, but the failure is a hard boot error from an unrelated SPI.

### The Problem
```java
public interface OAuth2GrantTypeFactory extends ProviderFactory<OAuth2GrantType> {
    String getShortcut();
}
```
`DefaultTokenContextEncoderProviderFactory.postInit` (lines 70-78) calls `gtf.getShortcut()` on every registered `OAuth2GrantType` factory and puts the result into a `ConcurrentHashMap`. `DefaultKeycloakSessionFactory` runs `postInit` during startup and does not catch the exception.

### Theory / Proof
- Calling an interface method that a pre-compiled implementation does not provide throws `java.lang.AbstractMethodError` (JLS 13.5.3 / JVMS invokeinterface).
- `ConcurrentHashMap.put` rejects null keys and values with an NPE, so a factory returning `null` also aborts startup. `getShortcutByGrantType` (lines 112-114) has the same problem.

### What happens today vs. what will break
All in-tree factories implement the method. `OAuth2GrantTypeSpi.isInternal()` is `true` and the interface is in `server-spi-private`, so breaking it is allowed by Keycloak policy. Even so, an upgraded deployment with an existing custom grant JAR fails to boot, with an error from the token-context encoder rather than from its own provider. That is hard to diagnose.

### Recommendation
Either add a migration-guide note, or degrade gracefully:
```java
default String getShortcut() {
    return null; // not encodable; encoder falls back to "na"
}
```
```java
String grantShortcut = gtf.getShortcut();
if (grantShortcut == null) {
    logger.warnf("Grant type '%s' does not provide a shortcut; tokens will use the unknown grant", grantName);
    return;
}
```
Also treat a missing shortcut as `UNKNOWN` in `getShortcutByGrantType`, so that `encodeTokenId` does not throw on every token for that grant.

## [ISSUE] Shortcut length is not enforced; Javadoc says 3 letters but codec requires exactly 2

**Severity:** MEDIUM - a grant implementer who follows the Javadoc produces tokens whose ids cannot be decoded.

### The Problem
Javadoc on the new SPI method:
```java
 * @return usually like 3-letters shortcut of specific grants. ...
String getShortcut();
```
Decoder in `DefaultTokenContextEncoderProvider.getTokenContextFromTokenId` (line 74-81):
```java
if (encodedContext.length() != 6) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Incorrect token id: '" + encodedTokenId + "'. Expected length of 6.");
}
String gtShortcut = encodedContext.substring(4, 6);
```
The encoder (`encodeTokenId`) concatenates whatever the factory returns, and `DefaultTokenContextEncoderProviderFactory.postInit` only checks for duplicates, not length or characters. The PR description says the second commit relies on "assuming that every 'type' has always 2 characters long shortcut", but nothing enforces that assumption.

### Theory / Proof
With a custom grant returning `"abc"`: `encodeTokenId` gives `onrtabc:<uuid>` (7 chars), and the token is issued. Decoding throws `IllegalArgumentException("... Expected length of 6.")`. A shortcut containing `:` breaks the `indexOf(':')` split in the same way.

### What happens today vs. what will break
All in-tree shortcuts are 2 chars, so there is no failure yet. Once introspection/userinfo decode the id (#37662), every token from such a grant fails at the resource endpoint.

### Recommendation
Fix the Javadoc and fail fast at startup:
```java
/**
 * @return exactly 2-character shortcut of this grant, unique across grants ...
 */
String getShortcut();
```
```java
// DefaultTokenContextEncoderProviderFactory.postInit
if (grantShortcut == null || grantShortcut.length() != 2 || grantShortcut.indexOf(':') != -1) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Grant '" + grantName + "' has invalid shortcut '" + grantShortcut + "'. Must be exactly 2 characters.");
}
```

## [ISSUE] Decoding throws on still-valid tokens when a grant's feature is disabled

**Severity:** MEDIUM - a correctly signed, unexpired token can make the decoder throw a RuntimeException.

### The Problem
```java
String gt = factory.getGrantTypeByShortcut(gtShortcut);
if (gt == null) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Incorrect token id: " + encodedTokenId + ". Unknown value '" + gtShortcut + "' for grant type");
}
```
`grantsByShortcuts` is built only from factories returned by `getProviderFactoriesStream(OAuth2GrantType.class)`. Several grant factories are `EnvironmentDependentProviderFactory` and exist only while their feature is on: `CibaGrantTypeFactory` (`ci`), `DeviceGrantTypeFactory` (`dg`), `TokenExchangeGrantTypeFactory` (`te`), and `PreAuthorizedCodeGrantTypeFactory` (`pc`, OID4VC_VCI). The same applies when a custom grant provider JAR is removed, or during a rolling upgrade where one node has a grant the others do not.

### Theory / Proof
1. Issue a device-flow token: its id is `onrtdg:<uuid>`.
2. Restart with `--features-disabled=device-flow` (or roll back a node). The token is still valid (signature, exp).
3. `getGrantTypeByShortcut("dg")` misses. The fallback stream finds no factory, returns null, and the decoder throws `IllegalArgumentException`.

In contrast, legacy ids without `:` degrade gracefully to `UNKNOWN` (see `testOldToken`).

### What happens today vs. what will break
There is no production caller of `getTokenContextFromTokenId` yet. When follow-ups use it in introspection/userinfo/admin REST, the endpoint returns a 500 for these tokens instead of treating the context as unknown and falling back to the current lookup logic.

### Recommendation
Treat unknown parts as unknown rather than as fatal:
```java
String gt = factory.getGrantTypeByShortcut(gtShortcut);
if (gt == null) {
    logger.debugf("Unknown grant shortcut '%s' in token id '%s'", gtShortcut, encodedTokenId);
    gt = UNKNOWN;
}
```
Also document on `TokenContextEncoderProvider.getTokenContextFromTokenId` that callers must be able to handle `UNKNOWN`.
